        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right)^2\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Put $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Expand the square and combine the squared terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2 &=\\sin^2 x+2\\sin x\\cos x+\\cos^2 x \\\\ &=1+2\\sin x\\cos x \\\\ &=1+\\sin(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$ and $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin(2x)$.\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and double the whole angle: $2x=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. The result is\n\n$$\n1+\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) .\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
